%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DB92D4D1] ### **Controlling the Fan**
%[text] 
%[text] **Stateflow** provides a graphical language that includes state transition diagrams, flow charts, state transition tables, and truth tables. Our application will use Stateflow to determine when the fan should turn on and off.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:5c5a)
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4195) **Exercise.** Using the **Library Browser**, search for and add a Stateflow **Chart** block.
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
  CheckInsertChart %[control:button:85e6]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] The fan control should be based on the temperature, connect
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:07d1) **Exercise.** Branch the temperature signal from the DHT block (T) into the Stateflow Chart.
  CheckConnectionDHT; %[control:button:788a]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4629) **Exercise.** Branch the Stateflow outflow signal to the fan block, call this outflow signal **FanSpeed.**
  CheckConnectionPWM %[control:button:1955]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] Present three ways to control the fan:
%[text] 1. **Simple Threshold value:** the fan turns on above a certain temperature and turns off below this same temperature
%[text] 2. **Hysteresis:** the fan turns on above a certain temperature and turns off below a lower temperature
%[text] 3. **Hysteresis + Debouncing:** the fan turns on above a certain temperature and turns off after the temperature goes below a lower temperature for some time \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Example control signals](text:image:1051)
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3131) **Exercise.** Based on the following plot, identify the different control algorithms used.
IdentifyControlSignal("Hysteresis + Debouncing",... %[control:dropdown:4bed]{"position":[23,48]}
"Hysteresis",... %[control:dropdown:58fb]{"position":[1,13]}
"Simple Treshold"); %[control:dropdown:4be3]{"position":[1,18]}
  %[control:button:86a1]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] Now that you understand the logic behind the three controls presented, can you identify their corresponding Stateflow implementation?
%[text] 
%[text] A)    ![](text:image:0f62)
%[text] B)    ![](text:image:05da)
%[text] C)    ![](text:image:5878)
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4882) **Exercise.** Based on the Stateflow chart above, identify the corresponding control algorithm used.
IdentifyControlStateflow("None",... %[control:dropdown:7fdb]{"position":[26,32]}
"None",... %[control:dropdown:16d0]{"position":[1,7]}
"None"); %[control:dropdown:36fe]{"position":[1,7]}
  %[control:button:7728]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] In the rest of this script, we will develop and deploy the hysteresis control algorithm.
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:5c64) **Exercise.** Open the Stateflow chart (double-click) and create the initial state. In this state, the fan should be off and so the FanSpeed is set to 0.
%[text] ![](text:image:46dc)
